  // ── PRIORITY RETENTION CONCEPTS (PSM II) ──────────────────────────────
  retentionGapConcepts: [
    {"label": "Coaching vs Teaching vs Mentoring vs Facilitating", "parentTopic": "Coaching, Teaching, Mentoring and Facilitating Stances", objective: "2.1", "keyword": "Scrum.org describes Scrum Master stances. COACHING asks questions so the team finds its own answers; TEACHING transfers knowledge the team lacks (Scrum, an engineering practice); MENTORING shares personal experience and advice over time; FACILITATING ensures a conversation or event gets its purpose achieved without the facilitator steering the content. Choose by the situation: a team that does not know a rule needs teaching, a team that knows but is stuck needs coaching. Telling the team the answer is a last resort for a self-managing team."},
    {"label": "The Four EBM Key Value Areas", "parentTopic": "Evidence-Based Management Key Value Areas", objective: "3.1", "keyword": "Evidence-Based Management uses FOUR key value areas. CURRENT VALUE is the value the product delivers to customers today; UNREALIZED VALUE is the value that could be gained if every customer need were met; TIME TO MARKET is how quickly the organization can deliver new capabilities and learn; ABILITY TO INNOVATE is how effectively it can deliver new capabilities that customers value. Current and unrealized value are about the market; time to market and ability to innovate are about the organization."},
    {"label": "Nexus Integration Team: Accountable for the Integrated Increment", "parentTopic": "Nexus Roles and Artifacts", objective: "4.3", "keyword": "In a Nexus (roughly three to nine Scrum Teams on one product) the NEXUS INTEGRATION TEAM is accountable for an Integrated Increment that is done at least once per Sprint. It consists of the Product Owner, a Scrum Master and Nexus Integration Team members. There is ONE Product Owner and one Product Backlog for the whole Nexus."},
    {"label": "Definition of Done Across Multiple Teams", "parentTopic": "Definition of Done and Technical Excellence", objective: "4.1", "keyword": "When several Scrum Teams work on one product, they must define and comply with a mutually agreed Definition of Done. In a Nexus, the Nexus Integration Team is accountable for the Definition of Done that applies to the Integrated Increment; a team may apply a MORE stringent standard to its own work but never a less stringent one. Differing standards hide unfinished work and make the Integrated Increment untrustworthy."},
    {"label": "Servant Leadership: Serving Without Managing or Abdicating", "parentTopic": "Servant Leadership", objective: "2.2", "keyword": "Scrum.org's Scrum Master stances use the term servant leader; the Scrum Guide 2020 itself describes the Scrum Master as a TRUE LEADER WHO SERVES: they put the team's and organization's needs first, remove impediments and coach, but they do not command, assign work or take decisions the Scrum Team owns. The two failure modes are managing (acting as boss, taking over the Daily Scrum) and abdicating (staying out of conflict or impediments to avoid interfering). Good practice sits between them: help the team help itself, and escalate organizational impediments the team cannot resolve."},
    {"label": "Self-Managing: Who, What, When and How", "parentTopic": "Self-Managing Teams", objective: "2.3", "keyword": "The Scrum Guide 2020 calls the Scrum Team SELF-MANAGING: it decides internally WHO does what, WHEN and HOW, rather than being directed by outsiders. Cross-functional means the team has all the skills needed to create value each Sprint. Self-management does not mean freedom from the Product Owner's ordering or the Sprint Goal: the team chooses how to deliver, the Product Owner is accountable for what has most value. The Scrum Master coaches self-management, not substitutes for it."},
    {"label": "Nexus Events Add Cross-Team Integration; Only the Review Replaces", "parentTopic": "Nexus Events and Integration", objective: "4.4", "keyword": "The Nexus Guide (2021) adds events on top of the Scrum events. CROSS-TEAM REFINEMENT is a Nexus event in its own right and keeps dependencies visible; NEXUS SPRINT PLANNING coordinates the work across teams and produces a Nexus Sprint Goal; the NEXUS DAILY SCRUM lets representatives inspect the state of the Integrated Increment and spot integration issues; the NEXUS SPRINT RETROSPECTIVE is held alongside each team's own retrospective. Only the NEXUS SPRINT REVIEW replaces the individual team Sprint Reviews, covering the Integrated Increment for the whole Nexus. The others supplement team events rather than replace them."},
    {"label": "Technical Debt Reduces Transparency and Future Value", "parentTopic": "Technical Debt and Quality", objective: "4.2", "keyword": "Technical debt is the cost of shortcuts that make future change slower or riskier. It matters in Scrum because it erodes the quality promised by the Definition of Done and the ability to innovate, and it hides in an Increment that looks done. The Developers are accountable for quality; the Scrum Master helps the team and organization see the cost; the Product Owner is accountable for value and must be able to weigh it. A sound response is to tighten the Definition of Done so new work does not add debt."}
  ],

